  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1115 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nRain chances continue to increase Friday into Saturday thanks to \nthe presence of deepening boundary layer moisture and an \napproaching southern stream disturbance along with a cold front of\nnorthern stream origins. While the disturbance aloft is progged \nto lift/shear northeastward throughout Friday, which would reduce \ndynamics aloft, the lingering/stalling front and anomalous \nmoisture axis will remain draped across Middle Tennessee into \nSaturday fostering additional rain shower activity. Instability \nvalues are low (MLCAPE <750 J/kg) due to saturated vertical \nprofiles and poor lapse rates, so storms should remain isolated. \nLatest guidance suggests the front will begin to progress \nsoutheast again on Sunday with influence of high pressure building\nto our north, with perhaps another reinforcing cold front moving \nin as well. The remainder of the forecast period (into mid next \nweek) would transition to cooler, stable weather.",
